<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sari, Iran</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sari is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a> province in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, on the plain between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alborz">Alborz</a> mountains and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. It was a seat of the Dabuyid ispahbads of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> and later of local dynasties such as the Bavandids and the Marashis. It remains an important agricultural and commercial centre of the Caspian lowlands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sari-iran.jpg" alt="Sari, Iran" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: matin fattahi · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%DB%8C%D8%AF%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%B3%D8%A7%D8%B9%D8%AA_%D8%B3%D8%A7%D8%B1%DB%8C_-_panoramio_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sari lies in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, between the northern slopes of the Alborz Mountains and the southern coast of the Caspian, here called the Mazandaran Sea. Excavations in the Hutto cave show settlements around Sari as far back as the 7th millennium BCE. The climate is humid subtropical, with cool, rainy winters and hot, humid summers, and the surrounding land is known for its gardens and citrus groves. Sari is the largest city of Mazandaran.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Muslim historian Hamdollah Mostowfi attributed the foundation of Sari to the king Tahmoures Divband of the Pishdadian dynasty, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdowsi">Ferdowsi</a> names the city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>. Local folklore says it was settled when the blacksmith Kaveh, a native of the city, revolted against the tyrant Zahak, and that Fereydun chose to live near him. The city may be the Zadracarta of Greek sources of the Achaemenid era, though other sources place Zadracarta at or near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gorgan</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> it was the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyrcania">Hyrcania</a>. It was again a regional capital in the Sasanian era.</p><p>In the seventh century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhan-the-great">Farrukhan the Great</a> of the Dabuyid dynasty rebuilt the city and named it after his son Saruyeh, and Sari again became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amol</a> had been before. After invasions it lost its high standing and was periodically burnt. Shah Abbas the Great settled large numbers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and other peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a>, and made Farahabad his alternate capital to the north of the city. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a> era the face of the town changed drastically, and the railway station and most of the streets and government buildings date from then.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sari is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a> province and of its county and district. Most of its people speak the Mazandarani language, with Persian as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a> from Sari who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 10th century founded a town called Navu Sari, now Navsari, still a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsi</a> community. The clock tower in the Clock Square, built in 1930, is a local landmark, and the tomb towers include the Imamzadeh Abbas. The economy rests on food production, citrus fruit, paper and wood, and Mazandaran Wood and Paper Industries has its works there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sari, Iran” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dynasty ruled in three main branches: the Kayusiyya (to 1074), the Ispahbadiyya (1074 to 1210), centred on <b>Sari</b> and Amol, and the Kinkhwariyya (1238 to 1349).</blockquote><a href="/state/bavand-dynasty">Bavand dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>His descendants, named after his son Dabuya, became the ispahbads of Tabaristan, ruling from <b>Sari</b> and the coastal lowlands.</blockquote><a href="/state/dabuyid-dynasty">Dabuyid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1359 they defeated the Afrasiyabids of Mazandaran, and Qavam al-Din's sons established a theocratic principality based at Amol and <b>Sari</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/marashi-dynasty">Mar'ashi dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
